-- LearnLoop advanced lessons: 6 skills, 36 lessons (Moderate, Hard, Expert), 96 quiz questions.
-- Safe to run more than once: anything that already exists is skipped.
-- Run in DB Browser for SQLite (Execute SQL), or: sqlite3 database/database.sqlite < advanced_lessons.sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

-- Conversational Spanish

Useful answers: Gira a la derecha (turn right), Gira a la izquierda (turn left), Sigue todo recto (keep going straight). Está cerca means it is close and Está lejos means it is far.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='Asking for directions');

hablar: yo hablo, tú hablas, él/ella habla, nosotros hablamos, vosotros habláis, ellos hablan. The same pattern works for trabajar, estudiar and caminar.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='Present tense of -ar verbs');

Use estar for location and for temporary states or feelings: Los libros están en la mesa. Estoy cansado (I am tired). Changing the verb can change the meaning: Soy aburrido means ''I am boring'', but Estoy aburrido means ''I am bored''.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='Ser vs estar');

Notice the accents on yo and él/ella forms: hablé, habló, comí, comió. They change the meaning, because hablo (present) and habló (past) are different words.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='The preterite (finished past)');

To form it, take the yo form of the present tense, drop the -o, and switch the vowel: hablar becomes hable, comer becomes coma. Some verbs are irregular: estar becomes esté, ir becomes vaya, venir becomes venga. Expressions of certainty such as Sé que or Creo que use the normal indicative.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='The present subjunctive');

Por points to a cause, a duration, an exchange or movement through a place: Gracias por tu ayuda. Estudié por dos horas. Pagué diez pesos por el libro. Camino por el parque.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Conversational Spanish','Spanish') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='Por vs para');

-- Intro to Python

Indexes start at 0, so fruits[0] is ''apple''. Use fruits[-1] for the last item, len(fruits) for the size, and fruits.append(''grape'') to add an item at the end.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='Lists');

def greet(name):
    return ''Hello '' + name

Call it with greet(''Ana''). The function runs, returns ''Hello Ana'', and ends.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='Functions');

Keys are unique. Assigning to an existing key replaces its value: ages[''Ana''] = 21. Loop over pairs with for name, age in ages.items().',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='Dictionaries');

with open(''notes.txt'', ''r'') as f:
    text = f.read()

Modes: ''r'' reads, ''w'' erases and writes, ''a'' appends to the end. To handle errors, put risky code in try and handle the problem in except, for example except FileNotFoundError.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='Files and exceptions');

A generator expression uses round brackets: (n * n for n in range(5)). It makes values one at a time instead of building the whole list, so it saves memory with big data.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='List comprehensions and generators');

class Dog:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name
    def bark(self):
        return self.name + '' says woof''

rex = Dog(''Rex'') creates an object. __init__ runs automatically when an object is created, and self means the object itself. A class can inherit from another: class Puppy(Dog) gets everything Dog has.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Intro to Python') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='Classes and objects');

-- Photoshop Basics

On a mask, white shows the layer, black hides it, and shades of gray make it partly transparent. Because nothing is erased, you can paint white again to bring the hidden part back.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='Layer masks');

By default an adjustment affects every layer below it. To limit it to the layer directly under it, create a clipping mask by Alt-clicking (Option-clicking on Mac) the line between the two layers.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='Adjustment layers');

Filters applied to a Smart Object become Smart Filters. They stay editable and can be turned off or changed later. Double-click the Smart Object to open and edit its contents.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='Smart Objects');

Overlay increases contrast by combining Multiply and Screen, and Soft Light does the same more gently. Color applies only the hue and saturation of the top layer.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='Blend modes');

For print, work at about 300 pixels per inch at the final size. For export, use JPEG for photos, PNG when you need transparency, PSD to keep layers, and TIFF for high-quality print files.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='Color modes and output');

With this split you can smooth blotchy skin color on the low layer and repair small blemishes on the high layer, while the natural texture stays intact. Over-smoothing the low layer or removing too much from the high layer makes skin look plastic.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Photoshop Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='Frequency separation');

-- Flutter Fundamentals

Wrap a child in Expanded to make it take the remaining space along the main axis. Two Expanded children share the space equally, or you can give them a flex value to split it in a ratio such as 2 to 1.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='Row, Column and Expanded');

To go back, call Navigator.pop(context). You can also send data back by passing a value to pop and awaiting the result of push.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='Basic navigation');

Changing a variable without setState does not refresh the UI. Keep build fast and free of side effects such as network calls, because Flutter may call build many times.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='setState and build');

Future<String> load() async {
  final text = await fetchText();
  return text;
}

await only works inside an async function. FutureBuilder is a widget that builds different UI for loading, error and done states of a Future.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='Future, async and await');

Provider makes that object available to the widgets below it. context.watch<T>() listens and rebuilds the widget when data changes. context.read<T>() reads the object once without listening, and is the right choice inside button handlers.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='State management with ChangeNotifier and Provider');

Use const constructors for widgets that never change, so Flutter can reuse them. Use Keys when items in a list can be reordered or removed and each item holds its own state; the key lets Flutter match each state to the right item.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Flutter Fundamentals') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='Performance: builders, const and keys');

-- Guitar Basics

Watch which strings to strum: for D, start on the fourth (D) string and avoid the two lowest strings. For A, start from the fifth (A) string. Let every string ring clearly before you change chords.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='Open chords: Em, Am, D and A');

Keep your strumming hand moving in a steady down-up motion even when you do not hit the strings. This keeps the rhythm even. Practice slowly with a metronome and speed up only when it is clean.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='Strumming patterns');

The E-shape barre chord moves up the neck: the barre at the 1st fret is F, at the 3rd fret is G, and at the 5th fret is A. Use the bony side of your index finger, keep your thumb behind the neck, and press evenly. Buzzing usually comes from uneven pressure or finger placement.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='Barre chords');

The first pattern, called box 1, sits around the 5th fret. Because the scale leaves out the notes that clash most with typical chords, it is hard to hit a bad note, which makes it ideal for improvising over a backing track.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='The minor pentatonic scale');

In Travis picking the thumb plays a steady alternating bass on the lower strings while the fingers pick the melody on the higher strings. Start slowly with just the thumb pattern until it runs on its own, then add the fingers.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='Fingerstyle and Travis picking');

For example, a G shape with the capo at fret 2 sounds as A, and an E shape with the capo at fret 5 sounds as A. To find the sounding chord, count the capo fret in semitones up from the shape''s name.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('Guitar Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='Using a capo and transposing');

-- UI/UX Design Basics

A persona is a fictional profile built from the patterns you find in real research. It gives the team a shared picture of who they are designing for, including goals and frustrations.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='User research: interviews and personas');

A progress bar during upload follows ''visibility of system status''. A confirmation step before deleting an account follows ''error prevention''.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='moderate') AND title='Usability heuristics');

Use the words your users use for labels, not internal jargon. Keep important items within a few clicks of the home screen.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='Information architecture and card sorting');

Give images alt text so screen reader users know what they show. Make touch targets large enough, and make sure everything can be reached with a keyboard or screen reader.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='hard') AND title='Accessibility basics');

Common measures: task success rate (the percentage who complete a task), time on task, and the System Usability Scale (SUS), a standard 10-question survey of perceived usability.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='Usability testing and metrics');

Name tokens by purpose, such as color-danger, rather than by value, such as red. Then a theme change only updates the token''s value, and every screen that uses it stays consistent.',COALESCE((SELECT MAX("order") FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert')),0)+1,'published','text',datetime('now'),datetime('now') WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lessons WHERE skill_level_id=(SELECT id FROM skill_levels WHERE skill_id=(SELECT id FROM skills WHERE title IN ('UI/UX Design Basics') ORDER BY id LIMIT 1) AND name='expert') AND title='Design systems and design tokens');

COMMIT;
